                        "content": "A client is admitted to an emergency department, and a diagnosis of myxedema coma is made. Which action would the nurse prepare to carry out initially?",
                        "subjectId": "ad5ab04c79144c0ea9b83385308c3b1d",
                        "difficultyIds": "80db17ea97ed8fdcd1b6b9666390fca1",
                        "difficulty": "考试范围-Applying 应用",
                        "textAnalysis": "Rationale:\nMyxedema coma is a rare but serious disorder that results from persistently low thyroid production. Coma can be precipitated by acute illness, rapid withdrawal of thyroid medication, anesthesia and surgery, hypothermia, and the use of sedatives and opioid analgesics. In myxedema coma, the initial nursing action is to maintain a patent airway. Oxygen needs to be administered, followed by fluid replacement, keeping the client warm, monitoring vital signs, and administering thyroid hormones by the intravenous route.\n\nTest-Taking Strategy:\nNote the strategic word, initially. All the options are appropriate interventions, but use the ABCs—airway, breathing, and circulation—in selecting the correct option.",
                        "id": "0df5fcae1ef943d48e9c38e6b263e935",
                        "mark": "0",
                        "collectionState": "0"
                    },
                    {
                        "optionList": [
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "5324d67e83e04705b0f8b2cb80c428dd",
                                "content": "It indicates nerve damage."
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "c809bb5cdb4a4dd48cffd24cb2c8be6b",
                                "content": "The hoarseness is permanent."
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "1",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "7240d5eccde14fcb8bbda784a634b654",
                                "content": "It is normal during this time and will subside."
                            },
                            {
                                "isRight": "0",
                                "userAnswer": "",
                                "isSelected": "0",
                                "id": "c84575bff5724748ab40236ad2bb4105",
                                "content": "It will worsen before it subsides, which may take 6 months."
                            }
                        ],
                        "extJson": {
                            "optionList": [
